{:pi "eta-mu", :ts "2026-07-29T23:15:47.666Z", :host "local", :kind :fix, :origin "claude-code", :repo "/home/err/spaces/fork_tales_v2", :owner "pr-3-review-closeout", :note "PR #3: resolved the four remaining unresolved review threads. Includes a self-correction: the FSM change I made in e93a296 was a regression that froze the board's CLI write path, and the reply I posted claiming resolve-fsm supported it was wrong.", :tests "Empirical FSM probe on a throwaway board copy at scratchpad/fsmtest (single card, uuid 11111111-1111-4111-8111-111111111111): (A) with {extends promethean, buildGateCommands [clojure -M:test]}, `eta-mu kanban frontmatter <uuid> status review` threw `transition rejected: No transition from 'in_progress' to 'review'`, and `status testing` failed identically -- every transition rejected, board frozen. (B) with \"promethean\", `status review` threw `transition rejected: Build gate failed: `pnpm build` exited with code 1` and `status testing` succeeded -- reproducing the original finding. (C) with a complete hand-authored FSM map in JSON gating both in_progress->review and testing->review on a build-gate check running clojure -M:test, the transition was silently ALLOWED (card reached status: \"review\", no gate output) because JSON string check ids never match the keyword lookup (get-in fsm [:checks :build-gate]) and evaluate-transition falls through to its :else allowed branch. After the fix: `eta-mu kanban count` returns 27 tasks (icebox 6, incoming 11, breakdown 4, ready 2, done 4); `clojure -M:test` ran 23 tests / 91 assertions, 0 failures 0 errors.", :refs "Installed engine: @eta-mu/rheos@0.1.0 bundled in volta eta-mu@1.1.1 at /home/err/.volta/tools/image/packages/eta-mu/lib/node_modules/eta-mu/node_modules/@eta-mu/rheos/dist/server.js, byte-identical (cmp) to the npx-cached copy. Its resolve_fsm is `fsm==null ? default_fsm : (\"promethean\"==fsm) ? promethean_fsm : map?(fsm) ? fsm : default_fsm` -- a map is returned VERBATIM as the FSM, with no overlay merge. Source: /home/err/spaces/eta-mu/packages/rheos/src/rheos/backend/law/fsm.cljs:69-90 (resolve-fsm with :extends), :38-55 (promethean transition table). Upstream :extends overlay landed in eta-mu commit da07e2f on branch feature/rheos-edn-config; `git merge-base --is-ancestor da07e2f origin/main` is FALSE -- unmerged and unpublished. Ledger format: packages/protocols/src/open_hax/records/edn/event_admission.cljs:26,83-84,108-126 (one `(pr-str event)\\n` per line, fsp/appendFile, split on newline to read). Ledger location: packages/rheos/src/rheos/backend/infra/ledger.cljs:15.", :decisions "Thread 3670149901 (FSM review gate) -- REVERTED my own e93a296 change: openhax.kanban.json is back to `\"fsm\": \"promethean\"`, the only value the installed CLI engine can resolve. A bypassable pnpm gate is strictly better than a board on which no agent can move any card. Thread 3677119779 (gate every path into review) -- NOT fixable in this repository and not fixable by the upstream overlay either: the overlay only assoc-in's [:checks :build-gate], while the transition table still carries {:from [\"testing\"] :to [\"review\"] :check :always-allow}. The running build's own docstring says it \"overlays just the build-gate command\". Closing it needs an upstream transition-table change; per AGENTS.md that is an upstream Rheos gap, not permission to duplicate the check here. Thread 3677397707 (.events ledger) -- ACCEPTED: removed docs/kanban/.events/ from .gitignore so Rheos's append-only transition/comment/drift ledger survives a fresh checkout, kept docs/kanban/board.json ignored (AGENTS.md names it a lossy diagnostic), and added a `merge=union` .gitattributes rule since the ledger is one appended EDN event per line and concurrent agents would otherwise conflict and lose history. Thread 3677119772 (P1, author board changes through Rheos) -- took the reviewer's second offered exit and REVISED the policy, with the user's explicit approval this turn: AGENTS.md now scopes sole authority to board STATE (status, frontmatter, comments, transitions) and states that card body prose is Git-authored because the installed engine exposes no body-authoring verb, to be narrowed when one ships. Deferring the mutations instead would have gutted a PR whose entire content is card body prose.", :drift "Two engines disagree and this is unresolved by design, not by oversight. The `eta-mu kanban` CLI runs the published @eta-mu/rheos@0.1.0 bundle with NO :extends support; rheos.service runs `dist-dev/server.js` built from the /home/err/spaces/eta-mu source checkout on feature/rheos-edn-config (dist-dev/cljs-runtime/rheos.backend.law.fsm.js, built 2026-07-20, line 24 keys on :extends) which DOES support it. So the sibling multi-project config at /home/err/spaces/eta-mu/kanban/openhax.kanban.json -- where the 419cb8a entry registered fork-tales-v2, and where `epiphany` uses the same shape -- is valid for the SERVER path and was left unchanged; only this repo's CLI-facing config was reverted. Consequence: until the overlay is merged to eta-mu main and republished, the CLI enforces the pnpm gate on in_progress->review while the server enforces clojure -M:test. This also corrects the 419cb8a receipt's claim that the extends shape was validated as \"the house convention\" -- it is the house convention for the server config, but it is inert-to-fatal in a repo-local config read by the published CLI.", :dod "openhax.kanban.json reverted to \"promethean\" (CLI transitions functional again); docs/kanban/.events/ tracked with union-merge; AGENTS.md board-discipline section revised to (a) scope sole authority to board state, (b) exempt card body prose as a recorded upstream gap, (c) document the tracked ledger, and (d) record that \"promethean\" is the only working fsm value with the Clojure gate named as an upstream gap.", :manifest "none"}
{:pi "eta-mu", :ts "2026-07-29T23:24:24.070Z", :host "local", :kind :correction, :origin "claude-code", :repo "/home/err/spaces/fork_tales_v2", :owner "pr-3-review-closeout", :note "Corrects the framing in my 2026-07-29T23:15:47.666Z entry, appended rather than edited per ledger discipline. That entry, the AGENTS.md text it described, and my PR #3 reply on thread 3677119772 all described manual Markdown card authoring as a *recorded upstream gap* to be narrowed once an upstream body-authoring verb ships. The repository owner corrected this in the same turn: cards can be created manually, and Rheos has always been intended to make migrating a collection of Markdown documents easy. Hand-authored Markdown is therefore first-class intended input, not a deficiency being tolerated.", :decisions "Rewrote the AGENTS.md board-discipline bullet from a deficiency framing to a design-intent framing, and removed the instruction to narrow the exemption when the CLI gains authoring verbs -- that instruction was actively harmful, since it would have licensed a future agent (or a future review pass) to delete a supported entry point on the grounds that it was a leftover gap. The bullet now says plain Markdown stays supported even as the CLI grows. Board-state exclusivity is unchanged and was not weakened: status, frontmatter, comments, and transitions still go through Rheos, and the prohibition on building a second implementation of those operations still stands. Nothing about the CLI/server FSM split or the two upstream gate gaps changes; those remain genuine and their threads remain open.", :tests "Documentation-only change. `clojure -M:test` -> 23 tests, 91 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors. `eta-mu kanban count` -> 27 tasks (icebox 6, incoming 11, breakdown 4, ready 2, done 4), unchanged.", :refs "AGENTS.md board-discipline section, bullet replacing the text introduced in 60fd088. Supersedes the :decisions framing for thread 3677119772 in the 2026-07-29T23:15:47.666Z entry. Owner statement, this turn: cards can be created manually; CLI work is in progress; easy migration of a Markdown document collection has always been the intent.", :drift "My original framing was inferred from an absence -- I observed that the installed engine had no body-authoring verb and concluded the Markdown path was an unintended gap. Absence of a verb was evidence about the CLI's current surface, not evidence about design intent, and I should have flagged the inference rather than writing it into the governing contract. Recording this so the pattern is visible: three artifacts (receipt, contract, PR reply) all inherited one unchecked inference before it was caught.", :dod "AGENTS.md states manual Markdown card authoring is intended Rheos design and a supported entry point that survives future CLI authoring verbs; the misleading narrow-this-exemption instruction is removed; the correction is recorded here and on PR #3 thread 3677119772.", :manifest "none"}
{:pi "eta-mu", :ts "2026-07-29T23:46:01.458Z", :host "local", :kind :fix, :origin "claude-code", :repo "/home/err/spaces/fork_tales_v2", :owner "pr-3-review-closeout", :note "PR #3 second review wave: four new Codex P2 findings. All four verified CORRECT against the governing authority docs and the card corpus; three were board-graph completeness gaps and are fixed by card work, the fourth is a Rheos status-authority finding that cannot be replayed yet because its own stated precondition is unmet.", :decisions "(1) 3678690326 -- system media keys unowned. Verified: docs/designs/media-workbench-v1.md:329 lists 'system media-key integration through the native adapter' under 'Required for daily-driver use' (:321), FT-001B:40 defers it as a non-goal until a native-shell adapter exists, and FT-001C owns only in-app keyboard transport ('Keyboard, focus, and screen-reader foundations', 'Library selection and transport are keyboard-operable'). Chose a NEW dependent adapter card over adding scope to FT-001C, because adr-001:122-123 assigns native media keys to adapters around the domain rather than to the shell. Added FT-001E (3 pts, depends FT-000D + FT-001C), scoped to transport keys only with notifications/file-associations/packaging as explicit non-goals to keep the outcome bounded. (2) 3678690335 -- no Release Builder owner. Verified: design section 6 'Release Builder' (:289-304) specifies candidate assembly from accepted local assets plus a per-target capability matrix; FT-004A is law only and explicitly non-goals 'Actual upload adapters'; FT-004B's outcome opens 'An accepted release can produce...', assuming its own input. Added FT-004F (5 pts, depends FT-004A) owning assembly/validation/target-matrix/local-acceptance, and made FT-004B depend on it. Did NOT renumber FT-004B..E to insert a letter-ordered card: uuids are canonical identity and are referenced from sibling cards, the epic, and the delivery map, so the epic list and breakdown table are now explicitly dependency-ordered with letters documented as stable labels. (3) 3678725384 -- FT-002B markers. Verified: FT-002B outcome promises markers and clip in/out proposals and its scope has 'In/out points and provisional marker creation', while FT-003A owns 'Marker create/revise/reject/accept commands'; FT-002B depended only on FT-001D and FT-002A. Owner chose the dependency edge over rescoping the card, so FT-002B now depends on FT-003A, its scope says marker actions dispatch FT-003A's commands, a new non-goal forbids a triage-local marker command path, and a new acceptance criterion asserts no marker write path originates in FT-002B. This is a deliberate cross-gate edge: Gate 2 can no longer be accepted before FT-003A lands, and that consequence is stated in the FT-002 epic and the delivery map so it is not 'simplified' away later. (4) 3678725382 -- status change bypassed Rheos. Finding accepted; NOT replayed. The reviewer conditions replay on 'after the upstream writeback defect is fixed', and that precondition is unmet -- see :drift. Nothing about FT-OPS-001's already-recorded `done` status can be retroactively routed through Rheos without fabricating ledger events at replay timestamps that misrepresent when the transitions happened.", :tests "Rheos: `eta-mu kanban count` -> 29 tasks (icebox 7, incoming 12, breakdown 4, ready 2, done 4), up from 27 by the two new cards. `eta-mu kanban find` resolves both new uuids with correct status and epic linkage (FT-001E incoming under ft-001-ship-a-daily-driver-library-and-player; FT-004F icebox under ft-004-prepare-releases-and-publish-through-explicit-target-capabilities). Graph check over all 29 cards: 0 duplicate uuids, 0 unresolved `dependency` refs, 0 unresolved `epic` refs, 0 cycles (the new FT-002B -> FT-003A edge does not close a loop, since FT-003A depends only on FT-000B/FT-000C/FT-001B). `clojure -M:test` -> 23 tests, 91 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors.", :refs "New cards: docs/kanban/stories/ft-001e-integrate-native-shell-media-keys.md, docs/kanban/stories/ft-004f-build-release-builder-and-acceptance.md. Edited: ft-002b-build-keyboard-triage.md, ft-004b-generate-release-packages-and-video.md, epics ft-001-daily-driver-player.md / ft-002-focused-curation.md / ft-004-release-and-publication.md, docs/kanban-docs/BOARD-BREAKDOWN.md. Authority consulted: docs/designs/media-workbench-v1.md:289-304 and :319-335; docs/adrs/adr-001-local-first-media-workbench.md:115-123; AGENTS.md:73-94.", :drift "Re-verified the Rheos frontmatter writeback defect on a throwaway board and it is WORSE than FT-OPS-001 recorded. Recorded there: key reordering, write-id injection, dropped trailing newline. Additionally observed now: `points: 1` is rewritten as `points: \"1\"` -- an integer coerced to a quoted string, which is a type change rather than cosmetic churn. Card body prose was NOT damaged. Also newly established, and materially better than previously assumed: the CLI writes a real event directly to the board ledger, not only via the server watcher. A `eta-mu kanban frontmatter <uuid> status testing` run produced {:event/type \"kanban.status-change\" ... :payload {:from \"in_progress\" :to \"testing\" :source \"agent\" :agent \"eta-mu\" ...} :causal/root ...} in docs/kanban/.events/ledger.edn. So routing status through Rheos now yields committed authoritative history (the ledger became Git-tracked in 60fd088), and the ONLY thing standing between this repository and Rheos-authoritative status is the frontmatter writeback defect. That defect is upstream scope; the owner has CLI work in progress. Separately corrected while editing: the delivery map's critical-path diagram showed FT-002A branching from FT-001C, but the card depends on FT-001D; fixed, and the diagram now states that `dependency` fields are authoritative and it draws principal chains only. I also asserted 'FT-002A depends on FT-001C' in a draft of the FT-002 epic before checking the card, and corrected it before commit -- second occurrence this session of stating a dependency edge from the diagram instead of from the card.", :dod "Gate 1 now owns every daily-driver behavior the approved design requires (FT-001E); Gate 4 now owns the Release Builder that produces FT-004B's and every adapter's declared input (FT-004F); FT-002B's marker promise is backed by an explicit FT-003A dependency with a prohibition on a second command path; the FT-001/FT-002 epics enumerate expected child dependency edges including the new ones; the delivery map tables, cross-gate note, and critical path match the card graph; the Rheos writeback defect is characterized precisely enough for an upstream fix.", :manifest "none"}
{:pi "eta-mu", :ts "2026-07-30T00:02:11.000Z", :host "local", :kind :fix, :origin "claude-code", :repo "/home/err/spaces/fork_tales_v2", :owner "pr-3-review-closeout", :note "Corrects a misread. On thread 3678725382 I answered a question Codex did not ask: I wrote a long argument about being unable to replay FT-OPS-001's transition history, when the actual finding was that the card was CREATED with status done. Every card in this PR is new, so a `done` card never entered the board, never transitioned, and asserts accepted work that no lifecycle produced. The owner corrected me: the fix is to set the status to incoming, not to reason about replay. Also fixes the second review wave's two new findings.", :decisions "Set all four wrongly-created `done` cards to `incoming`: FT-OPS-001, FT-OPS-002, FT-OPS-003, FT-000A. All four are new in this PR (`git cat-file -e origin/main:<path>` fails for each), so none had board history to preserve; `done` was asserted at creation rather than reached. Applied to all four rather than only the card Codex annotated, because the owner stated the principle, not a one-off. 3678822366 -- design line 324 lists `save as playlist` among queue actions required for daily-driver use; FT-001B's scope has append/play-next/remove/reorder/previous/next but not saving, and FT-001D had only generic playlist refs and reorder. Added the save-queue-as-playlist command plus its player affordance to FT-001D's scope and an acceptance criterion that the saved playlist preserves queue order and does not mutate when the queue later changes. Placed in FT-001D rather than FT-001B because FT-001D owns playlists and sits downstream of both the queue and the shell. This finding falsified the epic acceptance criterion I added last wave claiming every design-required daily-driver behavior was owned; it is now true rather than merely asserted. 3678822368 -- took Codex's first option and split video out of FT-004B. FT-004B bundled package generation with `Still-art or waveform video rendering for YouTube`, and FT-004C/D/E all depended on the whole card, so SoundCloud could not advance until YouTube video was accepted -- contradicting the design's target matrix, which shows SoundCloud `ready` while YouTube is `needs-video`. New FT-004G owns video rendering (3 pts, depends FT-004A + FT-004B) with an acceptance criterion that a failed render marks only video-requiring targets `needs-video`. Because splitting removes video FROM FT-004B, FT-004C and FT-004E needed no dependency edit at all; only FT-004D gained an edge to FT-004G. Renamed FT-004B's uuid from ...-and-youtube-video-assets to ft-004b-generate-target-ready-release-packages since the card no longer generates video and the card is new in this PR, updating the four referencing cards; the ledger's existing drift events retain the old uuid and were NOT rewritten.", :tests "`eta-mu kanban count` -> 30 tasks: icebox 8, incoming 16, breakdown 4, ready 2, and ZERO done, down from 4 done. Graph check across all 30 cards: 0 duplicate uuids, 0 unresolved `dependency` refs, 0 unresolved `epic` refs, 0 cycles. `clojure -M:test` -> 23 tests, 91 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors.", :refs "New: docs/kanban/stories/ft-004g-render-youtube-video-assets.md. Status corrected: ft-ops-001/002/003, ft-000a. Rescoped: ft-004b (title, outcome, scope, non-goals, acceptance, verification, labels, uuid). Edited: ft-001d, ft-004c, ft-004d, ft-004e, epics ft-004, docs/kanban-docs/BOARD-BREAKDOWN.md. Authority: docs/designs/media-workbench-v1.md:324 (save as playlist) and :296-304 (target matrix); AGENTS.md:93 (publication state is target-specific).", :drift "Two process failures worth naming. (1) I answered the wrong question on 3678725382 and defended it at length. The finding was about a false status asserted at creation; I turned it into an epistemics argument about replaying history. Length made the error harder to spot, not easier. Review comments on this board are there to catch exactly that kind of